    "project_template_level_name": "template_building-data-driven-systems-lesson5-level1_choicea_2026",
    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n**Students may assume the displayed animals represent the entire dataset**\r\n\r\nSome students will treat the animals shown on the screen as if they are seeing all available data. Encourage them to compare the display to the full dataset by asking: **\"Which animals appear in the array but do not appear on the screen?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant idea:\r\n```javascript\r\nanimals array → displayed animals\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students recognize that apps often show only a subset of the available data.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may focus on what is shown and overlook what is missing**\r\n\r\nStudents often describe the visible animals without considering whether certain species, ages, genders, or statuses are absent. Encourage them to ask: **\"Who or what is not represented in the display?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant properties:\r\n```javascript\r\nspecies\r\ngender\r\nstatus\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis supports discussions about representation and selection.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may assume the display is neutral**\r\n\r\nSome students may believe the displayed animals provide a complete picture of the shelter. Encourage them to think critically by asking: **\"What impression would a user get if they only saw these animals?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant reflection:\r\n```javascript\r\nWhat might a user assume based on what is shown?\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students understand that the way data is displayed can influence perceptions.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may not connect the display to decisions made by the code**\r\n\r\nStudents may think the displayed animals were chosen randomly. Encourage them to investigate by asking: **\"What determines which animals are shown and which are left out?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant idea:\r\n```javascript\r\nFull dataset → selection process → displayed animals\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis prepares students to think about filtering, algorithms, and how technology can shape the information users see."
  },
